# Two tests that had bet on resources they do not own, 2026-10-04

`docs/ci-test-debt.md` carried a section describing two tests whose premise is a resource
they never held: one needed a port number to stay unallocated for 250 ms, which the kernel
is free to hand to someone else the moment the test releases it, and one needed a busy
4-core machine to finish eight seconds of protocol work inside eight seconds of wall time.
Both had actually failed -- the port one twice on this machine while an unrelated mutation
battery was running, the clock once on CI's runner and green again on the next commit. This
fixes both, measures the hazard each one was sitting in rather than asserting it, and says
plainly which of the two conditions could not be reproduced here.

== 0. What each one looked like before

The port one, in `crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/remote/client.rs`, as it stood when this
batch started (the reservation and the wait):

    let probe = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0")
        .await
        .expect("a port to reserve");
    let addr = probe.local_addr().expect("addr");
    drop(probe);

    (… 2 lines elided here: a blank line and `let appear = tokio::spawn(async move {`)

    tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(250)).await;
    let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind(addr).await.expect("the reserved port comes free");

The dial that follows may take up to ten seconds (`DialRetry::new(Duration::from_secs(10))`),
and the load-bearing assertion is the one after it:

    assert!(
        elapsed >= Duration::from_millis(200),
        "at least one retry happened: the port was not there first time"
    );

Everything rests on the address being closed at the moment the dial starts. `drop(probe)`
returns the number to the kernel, so it is closed only until something else is handed the
same number and starts listening.

The clock one, `crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/tests.rs`:

    let deadline = tokio::time::Instant::now() + WAIT_TIMEOUT + WAIT_TIMEOUT;
    while tokio::time::Instant::now() < deadline {
        if let Some(summary) = drain_lsp_diagnostics(mgr, std::time::Duration::from_millis(500))
            .await
            .filter(|summary| summary.text.contains(needle))
        {
            return summary.text;
        }
        tokio::time::sleep(std::time::Duration::from_millis(25)).await;
    }
    panic!("no summary mentioning {needle:?} within the deadline");

`WAIT_TIMEOUT` is 4 s, so the budget is 8 s of wall time for a flow whose every round is
one `drain_lsp_diagnostics` (which blocks at most its own 500 ms, and only while a server
still owes an answer) plus a 25 ms breath.

== 1. The port hazard is not hypothetical: it is the range `bind(:0)` allocates from

`drop` gives the number back to the ephemeral pool, and on Linux that pool is exactly the
range `bind(:0)` draws from, so a sibling test binary asking the kernel for a port can be
given this one. The pool and the shape of the allocation, measured rather than quoted from
memory:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    > import socket
    > lo, hi = (int(x) for x in open("/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range").read().split())
    > outside = 0
    > for _ in range(120):
    >     s = socket.socket(); s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0)); p = s.getsockname()[1]; s.close()
    >     outside += not (lo <= p <= hi)
    > print(f"ephemeral range {lo}-{hi}; bind(:0) draws outside it in {outside} of 120 rounds")
    > PY

    ephemeral range 32768-60999; bind(:0) draws outside it in 0 of 120 rounds

Then the shape of the test itself, replayed while other processes do what sibling test
binaries do -- hand out ephemeral ports and give them back -- and count how often a foreign
listener is sitting on the reserved port at the moment the dial would start:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    > import socket, sys, threading, time
    > LOW, HIGH = [int(x) for x in
    >              open("/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range").read().split()]
    > NAMED = int(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) > 1 else 0
    > ROUNDS, CHURN = 40, 8
    > found, lost, stop = [], [], threading.Event()
    >
    > def churn():
    >     """Hand out and give back ephemeral ports, the way sibling test binaries do."""
    >     while not stop.is_set():
    >         socks = []
    >         for _ in range(200):
    >             s = socket.socket(); s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0)); s.listen(1); socks.append(s)
    >         time.sleep(0.05)
    >         for s in socks:
    >             s.close()
    >
    > def reserve(port):
    >     """Reserve, drop, sleep 250 ms, then look: the shape of the old test."""
    >     probe = socket.socket(); probe.bind(("127.0.0.1", port)); got = probe.getsockname()[1]
    >     probe.close()
    >     time.sleep(0.25)
    >     peer = socket.socket(); peer.settimeout(0.2)
    >     try:
    >         peer.connect(("127.0.0.1", got)); found.append(got)
    >     except OSError:
    >         pass
    >     peer.close()
    >     back = socket.socket()
    >     try:
    >         back.bind(("127.0.0.1", got))
    >     except OSError:
    >         lost.append(got)
    >     back.close()
    >
    > threads = [threading.Thread(target=churn) for _ in range(CHURN)]
    > for t in threads:
    >     t.start()
    > for _ in range(ROUNDS):
    >     reserve(NAMED)
    > stop.set()
    > for t in threads:
    >     t.join()
    > mode = f"named port {NAMED}" if NAMED else "bind(:0)"
    > print(f"ephemeral range {LOW}-{HIGH}; mode {mode}; rounds {ROUNDS}")
    > print(f"rounds where a foreign listener held the reserved port: {len(found)}")
    > print(f"rounds where the reserved port could not be taken back: {len(lost)}")
    > PY

Run twice: once with no argument, which is the old shape (reserve with `:0`), and once as
`python3 - 21787 <<'PY'`, which is the port this test now uses.

    ephemeral range 32768-60999; mode bind(:0); rounds 40
    rounds where a foreign listener held the reserved port: 3
    rounds where the reserved port could not be taken back: 3

    ephemeral range 32768-60999; mode named port 21787; rounds 40
    rounds where a foreign listener held the reserved port: 0
    rounds where the reserved port could not be taken back: 0

Three rounds in forty is the old test failing: in those rounds the first dial succeeds,
`elapsed` is a few hundred microseconds, and the 200 ms assertion fires. The 250 ms sleep is
the whole exposure window, and this probe fills it with ordinary port churn. The same probe
on 21787 saw nothing in forty rounds, because that number is outside the range the kernel
allocates from: Linux starts at 32768, macOS and Windows at 49152.

== 2. The fixed test, under the same churn

The test now names its port (constant `LATE_TRANSPORT_PORT = 21787` in
`crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/remote/client.rs`, with the reason in its doc comment) and
probes it first, so a machine where something else really does use that port fails with
`port 127.0.0.1:21787 has to be free to test anything` instead of passing for a reason the
test cannot see. That command line was then run fifteen times while the churn above was
running, with the old-shape probe running once alongside the whole set:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib remote::client::tests::a_transport_that_comes_up_later_is_waited_for

    (… the line above, run fifteen times; every run printed the line below)

    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 191 filtered out; finished in 0.30s
    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 191 filtered out; finished in 0.30s

    (… 13 lines elided here: thirteen more runs, 1 passed and 0 failed each, all ~0.30 s)

    concurrent probe (old shape, bind :0), running alongside those fifteen runs:
    ephemeral range 32768-60999; mode bind(:0); rounds 40
    rounds where a foreign listener held the reserved port: 5
    rounds where the reserved port could not be taken back: 5
    fixed test under churn: 15 passed, 0 failed

Those two halves are one wall-clock interval: while the old shape was losing 5 of its 40
reserved ports to foreign listeners, the named-port test went 15 for 15.

== 3. The test still tests the wait

A test that waits for something can go green by not waiting at all, so the shipped retry
window in `retry_dial` was told that it is already spent on the first failure, which gives
the dial exactly one attempt. The patch is one expression inside the `Err(error @
RemoteError::Io { .. })` arm, applied once, and the driver aborts before touching the source
if the baseline is not green:

    - let Some(wait) = retry.delay(failures, started.elapsed()) else {
    + let Some(wait) = retry.delay(failures, started.elapsed() + Duration::from_secs(3600)) else {

The same command line either way, first unmutated then mutated:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib remote::client::tests::a_transport_that_comes_up_later_is_waited_for

    test result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 191 filtered out; finished in 0.30s

(with the mutant compiled in:)

    thread 'remote::client::tests::a_transport_that_comes_up_later_is_waited_for' (3900427) panicked at crates/codegen/chaos-engine/src/remote/client.rs:2327:18:
    the dial should keep trying until the port is there: Io { context: "connect 127.0.0.1:21787 (still failing after 1 attempts in 137.602µs)", reason: "Connection refused (os error 111)" }
    test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 191 filtered out; finished in 0.00s

KILLED, and `restore byte-identical: True` from the `cmp` the driver runs on every path. The
panic is the test's own `.expect`, and the attempt count in the error text is what the
mutant changed, so the flow is genuinely going through `retry_dial`'s window check.

== 4. The clock one: what 8 seconds of wall time was buying

The one observed CI failure, `rust check / clippy / test` run 37194865831, and green on the
next commit (`6826fce0`, same job):

    thread 'implementations::lsp::tests::a_refresh_we_cannot_act_on_does_not_discard_what_we_know'
    panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/lsp/tests.rs:86:5:
    no summary mentioning "a real problem" within the deadline

The thing being waited for is a python subprocess: it has to start the interpreter, finish
the LSP handshake, publish diagnostics on `didOpen`, and answer `workspace/diagnostic/
refresh`. None of that is slow by design; all of it is CPU the runner is also spending on
the other test binaries of the workspace.

Two ways of reproducing that on this 28-core machine were tried, both against the old
wall-clock helper and against the new one, and neither reproduced the failure. The labels
`[wall-clock]` and `[rounds]` below are the two shapes of `drain_until_reported`, the second
being the one this batch commits; the whole module was pinned to four CPUs with
`taskset -c 0-3` and `--test-threads=4`, with eight busy loops pinned to the same four CPUs:

    [rounds] round 1: exit=0 seconds=14.9
        test result: ok. 130 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3083 filtered out; finished in 14.89s
    [wall-clock] round 1: exit=0 seconds=14.9
        test result: ok. 130 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3083 filtered out; finished in 14.86s

    (… 4 lines elided here: rounds 2 and 3 of both shapes, all exit=0, 130 passed, 0 failed)

    == rounds: 3/3 rounds green, failures: []
    == wall-clock: 3/3 rounds green, failures: []

and harder, with the single LSP test module pinned to one CPU with four busy loops on it:

    [wall-clock] round 1: exit=0 seconds=15.6
        test result: ok. 53 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3160 filtered out; finished in 15.58s

    (… 4 lines elided here: rounds 2 and 3 of both shapes, all exit=0, 53 passed, 0 failed)

    == wall-clock: 3/3 rounds green, failing rounds: []
    == rounds: 3/3 rounds green, failing rounds: []

So the mechanism behind the CI red stays unconfirmed here. What the runs do show is the
irrelevance of the quantity the old helper was written against: both budget shapes took
~15 s per module round under four-way starvation and stayed green, so the failure was not
"the machine could not do the work in 8 s" in any shape this host can produce.

== 5. The clock one: the ceiling, from the flows' own behaviour

Since the machine's slowness could not be measured, the flows' own demand was. The helper was
temporarily told to say which round it returned on -- `for _ in 0..DRAIN_ROUNDS {` became
`for round in 1..=DRAIN_ROUNDS {`, with an `eprintln!` of `round` before the return -- and the
module was run five times, three plain and two pinned to four CPUs with `taskset -c 0-3`, each
run being the test binary cargo had just built under the filter
`implementations::lsp::tests::` with `--nocapture`:

    [plain 1] exit=0
        test result: ok. 53 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3160 filtered out; finished in 10.03s
        drains used: 1 for a real problem
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 2 for refresh answered=
        drains used: 2 for found once the solution was loaded

    (… 28 lines elided here: plain rounds 2 and 3 and pinned round 1, same 7 lines each)

    [pinned-4-cpu 2] exit=0
        test result: ok. 53 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored; 0 measured; 3160 filtered out; finished in 13.75s
        drains used: 2 for refresh answered=
        drains used: 1 for a real problem
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 1 for the check that only the push channel runs
        drains used: 2 for found once the solution was loaded
    restore byte-identical: cmp exit=0

Thirty-five completions across the five rounds, the largest at round 2. The committed budget
is `DRAIN_ROUNDS: usize = 15`, more than seven times that, and also the number of rounds the
old 8 s budget bought on an idle machine at ~525 ms a round -- so the ceiling sits where the
passing runs left it, in a unit the runner cannot spend on something else. The doc comment on
the constant carries the measurement; the instrumented source was restored with a plain `cp`
and confirmed with `cmp` (last line above) before anything else was run.

== 6. The clock one: the budget is live, and it fails fast

The shipped drain was told to swallow the summary it had just taken
(`take_answered_diagnostics` result dropped, `return None`), so no flow can ever see its
needle. Baseline first, and the four flows that call the helper:

    [baseline (unmutated)] exit=0 elapsed=0.7s
        test result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3211 filtered out; finished in 0.67s
    [mutated: drain swallows the summary it took] exit=101 elapsed=1.1s
        test implementations::lsp::tests::a_refresh_we_cannot_act_on_does_not_discard_what_we_know ... FAILED
        test implementations::lsp::tests::a_server_that_publishes_is_not_second_guessed_with_a_pull ... FAILED
        test implementations::lsp::tests::a_server_that_finishes_loading_late_still_gets_its_diagnostics_read ... FAILED
        test implementations::lsp::tests::a_diagnostics_refresh_request_is_answered_and_acted_on ... FAILED
        test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 4 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3211 filtered out; finished in 1.03s
    failing flows: 4/4
    panics naming the round budget: 4
        no summary mentioning "a real problem" in 15 drains
        no summary mentioning "found once the solution was loaded" in 15 drains
        no summary mentioning "refresh answered=" in 15 drains
        no summary mentioning "the check that only the push channel runs" in 15 drains
    KILLED
    restore byte-identical: cmp exit=0

Two things in that block are worth reading separately. All four flows failing says the
flows are genuinely driven through the shipped `drain_lsp_diagnostics`; and 1.03 s for four
flows that each spent their whole 15-round budget says the rounds are cheap when nothing is
coming, which is the case the old shape would have spent 8 s of wall time on per flow.

== 7. Reproduce it

The port one, in this checkout:

    $ cargo test -q -p chaos-engine --lib remote::client::tests::a_transport_that_comes_up_later_is_waited_for

To watch the old shape lose ports while the fixed test passes: run the probe from section 1 in
one shell (it starts its own eight churn threads; a second shell running just that `churn()`
loop over and over is closer to a real CI moment), and re-run the `cargo test` line above in a
third. The argument decides which port the probe reserves -- unset is the old shape, `21787` is
the one now in the test.

The clock one:

    $ cargo test -q -p xai-grok-tools --lib implementations::lsp::tests::

The two mutation drivers are the patches quoted in sections 3 and 6: back the file up,
replace the quoted anchor once, run the named tests, then `cp` the backup back and confirm
with `cmp`. Both drivers check the baseline is green before applying anything and restore on
the failure paths too.

== 8. What is still not known

The CI condition that made `a_refresh_we_cannot_act_on_does_not_discard_what_we_know` red
was not reproduced on this machine: four-way and single-CPU starvation both kept it green,
so this batch cannot say the new budget would have prevented that specific red. What it can
say is that the old helper's budget was denominated in something the runner is free to spend
elsewhere, that the new one is denominated in the exchanges the flows actually make, and that
the new one is measured to terminate: four flows spending their entire 15-round budgets took
1.03 s running concurrently (section 6), where the old deadline would have waited out eight
seconds per flow.

The residual risk of counting rounds instead of seconds is the opposite one, and it is
bounded: a flow that ever needs more than 15 rounds will now fail with a message naming the
count and the needle -- as it did in section 6 -- rather than silently waiting longer. That
failure is legible in a transcript in a way `within the deadline` was not.
